1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,028
Last lesson.

2
00:00:01,028 --> 00:00:02,044
The finale.

3
00:00:02,044 --> 00:00:04,440
Five milligrams, two vials.

4
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:06,744
One gets two milliliters of water.

5
00:00:06,744 --> 00:00:07,903
One gets five.

6
00:00:07,903 --> 00:00:09,765
Which vial holds more doses?

7
00:00:09,765 --> 00:00:10,598
Neither.

8
00:00:10,598 --> 00:00:12,356
Water never adds peptide.

9
00:00:12,356 --> 00:00:16,953
At two milliliters, two hundred fifty micrograms is ten units.

10
00:00:16,953 --> 00:00:21,093
At five milliliters, the same dose is twenty five units.

11
00:00:21,093 --> 00:00:23,033
Bigger draw, same dose.

12
00:00:23,033 --> 00:00:24,322
Now divide it out.

13
00:00:24,322 --> 00:00:29,296
Five thousand micrograms, at two hundred fifty each, is twenty doses.

14
00:00:29,296 --> 00:00:30,182
Either way.

15
00:00:30,182 --> 00:00:34,010
More water means more liquid per dose, never more doses.

16
00:00:34,010 --> 00:00:35,729
That was day ten of ten.

17
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:38,815
Ten days of peptide math, one lesson a day.

18
00:00:38,815 --> 00:00:40,442
And here is your next question.

19
00:00:40,442 --> 00:00:43,932
The moment that water went in, a clock started ticking.

20
00:00:43,932 --> 00:00:47,044
How long does a reconstituted peptide stay good?

21
00:00:47,044 --> 00:00:48,346
That's series two.

22
00:00:48,346 --> 00:00:49,817
The math doesn't lie.

23
00:00:49,817 --> 00:00:53,177
Buddy Pept dot com. Free, no pay wall.

24
00:00:53,177 --> 00:00:54,765
Follow so you don't miss it.

